{
  "requirement": "Each `AgentConfig` variant carries the corresponding settings from\n`trust_but_verify::agent`. Serde uses the `backend` field to select `bedrock`,\n`openai`, or `ollama` and reads the settings from the same object.\n\nChoose [`BedrockConfig`](#habeas-req-requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.BedrockConfig),\n[`OpenAiConfig`](#habeas-req-requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.OpenAiConfig), or\n[`OllamaConfig`](#habeas-req-requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.OllamaConfig) for the selected backend.\nWith settings omitted, OpenAI uses `gpt-6.1-sol`, `https://api.openai.com/v1`,\nand `OPENAI_API_KEY`. Ollama defaults to `http://localhost:11434`.\n\nAll three backend constructors start with an empty `options` map. Calling\n`option(key, value)` inserts one inference setting. Calling it again with the\nsame key replaces its value and preserves the other settings. An `OptionValue` is\na `String`, `Boolean`, `Integer(i64)`, `Float(f64)`, `Array`, or `Map`. Arrays hold\noption values and maps associate strings with option values. These serialize as\nthe corresponding untagged values. Backend calls reject nonfinite floats,\nincluding those nested in an array or map.\n\n`BedrockCredentials` contains `access_key_id` and `secret_access_key`, both\n`SecretString`, and an optional `session_token` of the same type. Construct a\n`SecretString` from `String` or `&str`. `expose_secret()` borrows its value.\nSerialization preserves the string, while `Debug` prints `<redacted>`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligations in `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.agent_config.settings` are all covered by the supplied roots. The rest is observable behavior or is delegated to refining requirements.\n\nCovered structural obligations:\n- **Variants and tag.** The text says \"Each `AgentConfig` variant carries the corresponding settings\" and that serde uses the `backend` field to select `bedrock`, `openai` or `ollama`. A flat struct, or a tag under a different name, would break this. `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#enum[AgentConfig]` declares `Bedrock(BedrockConfig)`, `OpenAi(OpenAiConfig)` and `Ollama(OllamaConfig)`. It uses `#[serde(tag = \"backend\", rename_all = \"lowercase\")]` with `openai` renamed.\n- **`OptionValue` variants.** The text says it is \"a `String`, `Boolean`, `Integer(i64)`, `Float(f64)`, `Array`, or `Map`\", with arrays holding option values and maps associating strings with option values. These serialize as the corresponding untagged values. `#[serde(untagged)] enum[OptionValue]` has exactly these variants, with `Vec<OptionValue>` and `BTreeMap<String, OptionValue>` for the nested cases.\n- **`BedrockCredentials` fields.** The text says it \"contains `access_key_id` and `secret_access_key`, both `SecretString`, and an optional `session_token` of the same type\". `struct[BedrockCredentials]` has those three fields with those types.\n- **`SecretString` interface.** The text says to \"Construct a `SecretString` from `String` or `&str`\" and that `expose_secret()` borrows its value. It also says serialization preserves the string. `struct[SecretString]` is a `#[serde(transparent)]` newtype. `impl[SecretString/From<String>]` and `impl[SecretString/From<&str>]` provide the two constructors. `impl[SecretString]/fn[expose_secret]` returns `&str`.\n\nNot structural, or covered elsewhere:\n- **Debug output.** `Debug` printing `<redacted>` is observable behavior. The implementation, `impl[SecretString/fmt::Debug]/fn[fmt]`, is also owned by the refining `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.SecretString`.\n- **Defaults and empty `options`.** The OpenAI defaults, the Ollama default and the empty `options` map at construction are observable results. Any construction that yields them satisfies the text.\n- **`option(key, value)` replacement.** The insert-or-replace semantics are likewise observable. The `option` methods and the `options` field types belong to the refining `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig` and `OllamaConfig` requirements. The settings text only says to choose those config types.\n- **Nonfinite-float rejection.** This is behavior, implemented by `impl[OptionValue]/fn[validate]`. The text prescribes no particular algorithm.\n\nNo further implementation roots are needed.\n\nbehavioral: The reviewed tests cover each behavioral obligation in `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.agent_config.settings`. They are all in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs` under `mod[tests]`.\n\n- **Backend selection and settings in the same object:** `fn[all_backends_preserve_nested_settings_and_credentials]` round-trips bedrock, openai and ollama JSON objects with a `backend` tag. It asserts the serialized value and the deserialized copy are equal. `fn[openai_inference_options_only_fill_missing_defaults]` deserializes `{\"backend\":\"openai\"}` and checks it equals `AgentConfig::OpenAi(OpenAiConfig::new())`.\n- **Defaults:** `fn[option_defaults_and_credential_resolution_are_deterministic]` asserts the OpenAI model `gpt-6.1-sol` and endpoint `https://api.openai.com/v1`. It also asserts the Ollama endpoint `http://localhost:11434`. `fn[openai_uses_the_default_credential_variable]` runs a child process with `OPENAI_API_KEY` set and asserts `OpenAiConfig::new().resolved_api_key()` returns that value. That is the correct way to test it, because it needs process environment state.\n- **Empty options at construction:** `fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` serializes freshly built configs. The output has no `options` key, so the options map starts empty.\n- **`option(key, value)`:** the same test chains `a=1`, `b=true`, then `a=2` on all three backends. It asserts the result is `{\"a\":2,\"b\":true}`, so a repeated key is replaced and the other key is preserved.\n- **`OptionValue` variants and untagged serialization:** the nested test round-trips a String, Integer, Float, Boolean, Array and Map. `fn[option_defaults_and_credential_resolution_are_deterministic]` checks that `1` deserializes to `Integer` and `1.0` to `Float`.\n- **Nonfinite floats rejected:** `options_json` on an Ollama config with a NaN nested in an array returns an error. `OptionValue::validate` on a map containing a nested infinity returns an error. The shared `AgentConfig::options_json` loops over every backend's options and calls `validate`. That is the check the backend calls rely on.\n- **`BedrockCredentials`:** `fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` checks serialization and deserialization with and without the optional `session_token`.\n- **`SecretString`:** `fn[configuration_debug_hides_credentials]` checks construction from `&str` and from `String`, `expose_secret()`, `Debug` printing `<redacted>`, and serialization preserving the string.\n\nI found no obligation that is untested or asserted wrongly, and I found no missing markers.",
